# Binaries and build output
*.exe
*.test
*.out
bin/
dist/
# A bare `go build ./cmd/naylampd` drops the daemon in the cwd; real gate builds
# target the ignored gate/out/. Anchored so it never matches the source packages
# engine/cmd/naylampd or engine/naylamp.
/naylampd
/engine/naylampd
# The demo binary the other main package drops, by the same accident and from the
# repo root. It is listed second because it was missed first: `go build
# ./engine/cmd/naylamp` left 7.8MB untracked AND unignored, which is worse than
# the daemon case, since it turns up in git status ready to be committed. Only
# the root path is anchored here, and there is no /engine/naylamp twin on
# purpose: that path is the source package, and from inside engine/ the build
# cannot land there anyway because the directory already holds it.
/naylamp

# Go
/vendor/
go.work.sum

# Node
node_modules/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Env / secrets
.env
.env.local
*.pem
# Private keys by NAME as well as by extension. The gate's current private key
# is named naylamp-gate-key-v2, with no suffix at all, so the *.pem above would
# not catch it if it were ever copied into the tree. The pattern is anchored to
# the key's own full name rather than to a -key stem: a pattern with no slash
# matches at any depth, so a stem rule would silently swallow any future source
# file whose name happens to contain it, engine/cluster/tls-key-loader.go being
# exactly the kind of file this repository would write next. The trailing star
# still covers -v3, -v4 and the pem without editing this list again. The
# exception keeps a public half trackable, since a .pub is not a secret; it is
# listed after the ignore so it wins.
naylamp-gate-key*
!*.pub

# Run artifacts: whatever a benchmark, sweep or gate writes into the tree. These are
# patterns rather than one line per file, so adding a benchmark or a gate does not
# mean editing this list again; name the output <something>_result.txt,
# <something>_bench.txt, or NAYLAMP_<NAME>_GATE<...>.txt and it is ignored already.
# The scripts and READMEs that PRODUCE these are committed; only their output is not.
#
# The first two lost their engine/ prefix on 4 August 2026, and the reason is the
# one the paragraph above already claimed and did not deliver. Anchored to engine/,
# the rule covered one directory rather than the repository, so the same convention
# followed anywhere else (services/, dashboard/, infra/, gate/) produced a file that
# was neither ignored nor swept by make clean, which is junk arriving without anyone
# choosing to keep it. Unanchored, a pattern with no slash matches at any depth,
# which is exactly the reach wanted here. The cost is stated rather than discovered
# later: a source file someone genuinely wants tracked and genuinely wants to name
# <something>_result.txt would now be ignored in silence. Nothing in the tree is
# named that way, the suffixes read as output to anyone who meets them, and a
# negation line wins over an ignore if that day ever comes.
*_result.txt
*_bench.txt
/NAYLAMP_*GATE*.txt
gate/out/

# Benchmark datasets, which live outside the repo
*.fvecs
*.ivecs
